{
  "schema_version": "3.0",
  "key": "modal_nuance",
  "concept_slug": "modal_nuance",
  "grounding_mode": "fallback_no_wiki",
  "title": "Modal Verbs and Stance Particles: Duty, Guessing, and Shared Knowledge",
  "cefr_level": "B2",
  "goal": "Choose and interpret må, kan, skal, vil, bør, vel, jo, nok, and da by meaning, not just by dictionary translation.",
  "elements": [
    {
      "kind": "section",
      "role": "orient",
      "title": "One Form, Two Jobs",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "A modal verb changes the meaning of another verb, often by adding duty, possibility, intention, or probability. In Norwegian, the same modal can point either to the real-world situation or to your judgment about what is likely."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "rule",
          "statement": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "Deontic meaning says what someone must, may, should, or intends to do. Epistemic meaning says what the speaker thinks is likely, possible, or expected."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "no": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "Du må levere rapporten i dag."
            }
          ],
          "en": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "You have to submit the report today."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "no": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "Hun må være på kontoret nå."
            }
          ],
          "en": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "She must be at the office now."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "The first sentence is about obligation. The second is an inference: you are not forcing her to be there, you are concluding that she probably is there."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "no": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "Du kan parkere her etter klokka fem."
            }
          ],
          "en": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "You may park here after five o'clock."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "no": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "Det kan bli glatt i kveld."
            }
          ],
          "en": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "It may get slippery tonight."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "section",
      "role": "contrast",
      "title": "Five Modal Verbs, Several Nuances",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "Use context to decide whether the modal is about action in the world or the speaker's assessment. The English gloss is only a guide; the Norwegian sentence decides the force."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "table",
          "col_langs": [
            "no",
            "en",
            "en"
          ],
          "headers": [
            [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "Modal"
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "Deontic use"
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "Epistemic use"
              }
            ]
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              [
                {
                  "kind": "text",
                  "value": "må"
                }
              ],
              [
                {
                  "kind": "text",
                  "value": "obligation or necessity"
                }
              ],
              [
                {
                  "kind": "text",
                  "value": "strong inference"
                }
              ]
            ],
            [
              [
                {
                  "kind": "text",
                  "value": "kan"
                }
              ],
              [
                {
                  "kind": "text",
                  "value": "permission or ability"
                }
              ],
              [
                {
                  "kind": "text",
                  "value": "possibility"
                }
              ]
            ],
            [
              [
                {
                  "kind": "text",
                  "value": "skal"
                }
              ],
              [
                {
                  "kind": "text",
                  "value": "plan, promise, rule, or intention"
                }
              ],
              [
                {
                  "kind": "text",
                  "value": "reported expectation or claim"
                }
              ]
            ],
            [
              [
                {
                  "kind": "text",
                  "value": "vil"
                }
              ],
              [
                {
                  "kind": "text",
                  "value": "wanting or willingness"
                }
              ],
              [
                {
                  "kind": "text",
                  "value": "prediction based on what seems likely"
                }
              ]
            ],
            [
              [
                {
                  "kind": "text",
